1
00:00:02,180 --> 00:00:07,980
宇宙之中存在着怪兽，它们能够一口吞下整个星球
There are monsters out in the cosmos that can swallow entire stars

2
00:00:08,090 --> 00:00:11,010
它们能够摧毁空间本身
that can destroy space itself.

3
00:00:12,490 --> 00:00:14,190
黑洞
Black holes.

4
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:17,650
几十年来，它们藏身宇宙，无人可见
For decades, they remained completely hidden.

5
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:19,620
可是现在
But now,

6
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:22,890
科学家们正向它们的领地进军
scientists are venturing into their uncharted territory.

7
00:00:22,890 --> 00:00:25,390
科学家们发现
They've discovered that black holes

8
00:00:25,390 --> 00:00:28,630
黑洞不仅统治着星球和星系
don't just rule the realm of stars and galaxies.

9
00:00:28,630 --> 00:00:31,400
它们甚至在影响着地球上的我们
They impact all of us here on Earth,

10
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:35,100
因为，黑洞也许正是那把
because black holes just might be the key

11
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:39,740
帮助我们理解真实自然的金钥匙
to understanding the true nature of reality.

12
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:50,320
空间，时间，生命本身
Space, time, life itself.

13
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:55,990
宇宙之谜，尽在虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie through the wormhole.

14
00:00:56,090 --> 00:00:59,090
♪穿越虫洞 第1x02集♪
♪through the wormhole 1x02♪

15
00:01:00,190 --> 00:01:05,200
黑洞之谜
The Riddle of Black Holes

16
00:01:06,730 --> 00:01:09,100
如果把地球
Take planet Earth

17
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:12,500
捏成一个玻璃弹球那么大
and squeeze it down to the size of a marble.

18
00:01:12,510 --> 00:01:15,810
你就创造了一个高密度的物体
You'll create an object so dense

19
00:01:15,810 --> 00:01:20,550
密度如此之大，即使是每秒能跑30万千米的光，
that not even light, traveling at 186,000 miles per second,

20
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:24,580
也无法从它的重力陷阱中逃脱
can escape its extraordinary gravitational pull.

21
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:26,420
它就叫做—
Its name --

22
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:28,150
黑洞
a black hole.

23
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:32,020
太空学家认为，当一个恒星
Astropsicists think that black holes might form

24
00:01:32,030 --> 00:01:34,260
耗尽了自身的燃料
when giant stars run out of fuel

25
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:37,800
也许就会坍塌成为黑洞
and collapse under their own weight.

26
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:41,630
我们并不十分肯定。为什么？
We're not really sure. Why?

27
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:43,800
因为黑洞是一个
Because black holes are places

28
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:48,140
已知的物理定律不管用的地方
where the accepted laws of physics break down.

29
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:51,810
一些勇敢的思想家试图解释黑洞内部发生着什么
A few bold thinkers are now making giant strides

30
00:01:51,810 --> 00:01:57,120
他们向着目标阔步前进
towards understanding what goes on inside black holes.

31
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:58,850
新出现的物理定律
And the new laws of physics that emerge

32
00:01:58,850 --> 00:02:02,150
带来了惊人的暗示
have an astonishing implication --

33
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:06,060
你，我，还有我们所居的整个世界
you, me, and the world we live in

34
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:09,800
也许只不过是一个假象
may be nothing more than an illusion.

35
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:21,010
在我的老家密西西比，曾有一个井
In my hometown in Mississippi, there was a well.

36
00:02:21,010 --> 00:02:23,610
我着迷于盯着那浑浊的井底
It fascinated me to gaze into its murky depths

37
00:02:23,610 --> 00:02:27,480
想知道那下面有什么东东
to try and see what lay at the bottom.

38
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:29,420
我坐在那里，向里边扔着鹅卵石
I would sit there, throwing pebbles into it

39
00:02:29,420 --> 00:02:33,320
极度渴望听到微弱的溅水声
and trying desperately to hear a faint splash of water.

40
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:36,690
可是只有寂静
But all I got was silence.

41
00:02:38,290 --> 00:02:42,060
一天，我拿了个“一元店”里的玩具兵
One day, I took a dime-store toy soldier,

42
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:45,060
用旧手绢给它做了个降落伞
made a parachute for it out of an old handkerchief,

43
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:47,730
然后看着它沉入井水
and watched it float down.

44
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:54,510
我想知道他触底后会发生什么
I wondered what would happen to him when he hit the bottom

45
00:02:54,510 --> 00:02:58,280
或者，他将永世沉沦
or if he would just keep on falling forever

46
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:01,980
落入那无尽黑暗
into that impenetrable blackness.

47
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:08,820
今日，理论物理学家对黑洞是“为伊消得人憔悴”
Today, theoretical physicists are drawn to black holes

48
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:10,890
就像我对古井
like I was to that old well,

49
00:03:10,890 --> 00:03:14,190
试图搞懂它们的原理
trying to understand how they really work

50
00:03:14,190 --> 00:03:17,630
希望它们能告诉我们宇宙的真理
and what they can tell us about the universe.

51
00:03:17,630 --> 00:03:20,030
这些情节听上去有点像科幻小说
It's one of those things that sounds like science fiction,

52
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:22,030
只不过，好点的是，你知道，这是真的。
only it's better because, you know, it's real.

53
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:24,840
Susskind:黑洞是通向未知世界的
Susskind: A black hole is the window into a world

54
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:28,640
一扇窗口
that we don't have the concept --

55
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:30,940
我们甚至没有合适的 思维结构去想象
we don't even have the mental architecture yet

56
00:03:30,940 --> 00:03:33,150
这样的情况
to be able to envision properly.

57
00:03:33,150 --> 00:03:36,980
你身处强重力的奇怪世界
You're in this strange world of strong gravity,

58
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:39,790
光线都绕着弯儿走
where there are no straight lines anymore.

59
00:03:39,790 --> 00:03:41,020
你甚至无缘得见
You can't even see it.

60
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:44,520
即令人兴奋又令人苦恼
That is disturbing and exciting at the same time.

61
00:03:45,860 --> 00:03:47,990
弗里曼:黑洞的概念
Freeman: The notion of a black hole

62
00:03:47,990 --> 00:03:50,030
是在引力定律的基础上 自然延伸出来的
is a natural extension of the laws of gravity.

63
00:03:50,030 --> 00:03:52,160
你离一个引力体越近
The closer you are to a massive object,

64
00:03:52,170 --> 00:03:54,400
引力的作用越强
the more the pull of its gravity

65
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:58,140
引力会把试图逃离的物体拉回去
slows down anything trying to escape from it.

66
00:03:58,140 --> 00:03:59,300
地球的表面
The surface of the Earth

67
00:03:59,310 --> 00:04:01,640
距离地心有4000英里（6000多千米）
is 4,000 miles away from its center.

68
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:04,780
所以地表的引力并不是那么强
So the force of gravity up here is not very strong.

69
00:04:04,780 --> 00:04:09,010
即使一个小娃娃，也能反抗成功1、2秒钟
Even a kid can resist it for a second or two.

70
00:04:09,020 --> 00:04:11,350
可是，如果你能把地球的质量
But if you could squeeze the Earth down

71
00:04:11,350 --> 00:04:14,390
全部挤到地心附近
so that all of its mass is really close to the center,

72
00:04:14,390 --> 00:04:18,660
引力就强的变态了
the force of gravity would grow incredibly strong.

73
00:04:18,660 --> 00:04:22,030
没有任何东西 能逃离它的表面
Nothing could move fast enough to leave its surface.

74
00:04:22,030 --> 00:04:23,560
别说是个蹦蹦达达的小鬼了
Not just a jumping boy --

75
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:25,800
就算是他鞋子上 发出的光也不行
even the beams of light speeding out from his shoes

76
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:27,600
都老实待着吧
would be trapped.

77
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:29,370
所以，如果你试图想象
So, if you're trying to imagine

78
00:04:29,370 --> 00:04:32,670
创造一个密度如此之大的物体， 即使光线也无法逃脱
creating something so dense that not even light can escape,

79
00:04:32,670 --> 00:04:35,070
你就要构造一个密实系统
you're trying to get a system so compact

80
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:38,010
它的逃脱速度
that the speed that it takes to escape from that object

81
00:04:38,010 --> 00:04:40,050
必须要比光还快
is greater than the speed of light.

82
00:04:40,050 --> 00:04:42,920
现在，真空中的光速是每秒30万千米
Now, the speed of light is 186,000 miles per second,

83
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:44,480
跑的真是快啊
so that's going really fast.

84
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:47,020
引力很微弱，我觉得它很惊人
Gravity's quite weak. I think it's surprising, you know.

85
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:49,120
地球用引力拉着宇宙飞船
The whole Earth is pulling on a rocket ship,

86
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:51,420
飞船要想逃离地球， 速度就要达到
and all it has to do is go 7 miles per second

87
00:04:51,430 --> 00:04:52,930
每秒钟7英里
to escape from the Earth.

88
00:04:52,930 --> 00:04:55,630
要创造一个黑洞
And to get all the way to a black hole,

89
00:04:55,630 --> 00:04:58,360
你要把太阳的所有质量
you'd have to crunch down the entire sun

90
00:04:58,370 --> 00:05:01,130
集中到几千米直径的球里
to be less than a few kilometers across.

91
00:05:01,130 --> 00:05:02,940
这样，如果想要逃脱
Now it would take something

92
00:05:02,940 --> 00:05:05,500
就要达到光速以上
traveling greater than the speed of light to escape,

93
00:05:05,510 --> 00:05:08,110
实际上，等于没有东西能逃脱 这个物体就变暗了
so nothing can escape, and the whole object goes dark.

94
00:05:10,810 --> 00:05:13,710
弗里曼: 天文学家Christian Ott
Freeman: Christian Ott, an astrophysicist

95
00:05:13,710 --> 00:05:16,180
供职于加州理工学院
at the California institute of Technology,

96
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:18,250
一直在努力搞明白
has been trying to understand

97
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:20,420
像黑洞这样的奇怪实体
how such strange entities as black holes

98
00:05:20,420 --> 00:05:23,260
是如何在宇宙中产生的
might really form in the cosmos.

99
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:25,020
他的研究是关于
He studies what goes on

100
00:05:25,030 --> 00:05:30,500
耗尽燃料，开始萎缩的大恒星
when giant stars run out of fuel and start to shrink,

101
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:31,900
这些恒星就像是
a process comparable to the collapse

102
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:36,070
耗尽了精力的，崩溃的马拉松运动员
of an exhausted marathon runner.

103
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:46,810
你可以把一个年轻的恒星
So, sometimes you can compare a star at the prime of its life

104
00:05:46,810 --> 00:05:50,320
比作一个刚出发的运动员
to a runner who's just starting out real fresh,

105
00:05:50,320 --> 00:05:52,420
充满活力和精力
consuming oxygen aerobically.

106
00:05:52,420 --> 00:05:54,290
恒星在燃烧
And it's the same with stars.

107
00:05:54,290 --> 00:05:57,260
核聚变把氢变成氦
They burn hydrogen into helium slowly,

108
00:05:57,260 --> 00:05:58,920
产生大量的能量
and they're getting a lot of energy

109
00:05:58,930 --> 00:06:01,060
烧掉每个氢原子核
out of every single hydrogen nucleus they burn.

110
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:03,230
[ 叹息 ]
[ Sighs ]

111
00:06:05,470 --> 00:06:09,070
当氢用完了，全变成氦了
After they're done fusing hydrogen into helium,

112
00:06:09,070 --> 00:06:11,870
反应开始产生更多的重元素
they go on to more and more heavy elements,

113
00:06:11,870 --> 00:06:14,170
燃料也消耗的越来越快
and that fuel goes fast and fast.

114
00:06:14,170 --> 00:06:16,710
最终，全变成了铁，烧不动了
So, at the end, they end up with iron,

115
00:06:16,710 --> 00:06:19,010
这时燃料没有了
and that's when their -- when their fuel is over,

116
00:06:19,010 --> 00:06:20,150
烧完了
their fuel is out.

117
00:06:20,150 --> 00:06:22,850
基本上像一个马拉松运动员
And it's basically like a marathon runner

118
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:24,680
在比赛里撞上了墙
hitting a wall in a marathon.

119
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:26,350
弗里曼:可是，不同的是
Freeman: But, unlike a runner

120
00:06:26,350 --> 00:06:29,120
运动员可以吃吃喝喝补充能量
who can restore his energy with food and drink,

121
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:32,360
濒死的恒星可没有加油站
a dying star has no way to come back from the brink.

122
00:06:33,830 --> 00:06:35,930
哎
Ugh.

123
00:06:35,930 --> 00:06:37,560
没有热量产生
There's no more heat generation,

124
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:40,030
在恒星核心不再有能量产生
no more energy generation happening at its core.

125
00:06:40,030 --> 00:06:41,670
引力一直在作用
So, gravity keeps on pulling in,

126
00:06:41,670 --> 00:06:42,900
当没有活动能够产生
and when there's nothing

127
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:44,670
支撑整个星球的压力
producing pressure to sustain it,

128
00:06:44,670 --> 00:06:46,010
崩溃就开始了
it will just collapse.

129
00:06:46,010 --> 00:06:49,340
震荡波传播出来
You get a shock wave, and the shock wave moves out.

130
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:51,510
整个星球突然爆炸
And it actually blows up the entire star,

131
00:06:51,510 --> 00:06:53,750
这一现象，我们称之为超新星
and that's the phenomenon we call supernova.

132
00:06:55,450 --> 00:06:58,280
弗里曼: 巨大星球的临死阵痛
Freeman: The death throes of giant stars

133
00:06:58,290 --> 00:07:02,020
是天文学家目击的最戏剧性事件
are the most dramatic events astronomers have ever witnessed.

134
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:06,130
中国占星家在1054年看到了一次超新星
Chinese stargazers saw one explode in 1054.

135
00:07:06,130 --> 00:07:10,500
它如此之亮，即使在白昼也清晰可见
It was so bright, they could even watch it by day.

136
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:14,370
400年前，又有2次爆发
Another two blew up around 400 years ago.

137
00:07:14,370 --> 00:07:16,240
巨大的爆炸
These colossal explosions

138
00:07:16,240 --> 00:07:18,870
留下了大量的气体和灰尘
leave debris fields of gas and dust

139
00:07:18,870 --> 00:07:20,870
分布于几光年半径的空间内
hundreds of light-years across,

140
00:07:20,870 --> 00:07:24,080
即使今天仍然可见，并且仍在扩展
still visible and still expanding today.

141
00:07:24,080 --> 00:07:27,280
但是，让黑洞研究者感兴趣的
But what interests black-hole researchers

142
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:28,950
并不是大爆炸
is not the explosion.

143
00:07:28,950 --> 00:07:32,850
而是在死亡恒星中心发生的事情
It's what happens at the very center of the dying star.

144
00:07:32,850 --> 00:07:34,720
现代天文学家
Modern astronomers

145
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:38,360
还无缘看到在我们星系里的超新星爆发
have never witnessed a star in our own galaxy explode.

146
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:42,560
但是理论物理学家预言，如果一个恒星足够大
But theoretical physics predicts that if a star is large enough,

147
00:07:42,560 --> 00:07:43,960
它的核心
its collapsing core

148
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:46,770
会坍塌成为黑洞
should shrink down to form a black hole.

149
00:07:51,270 --> 00:07:52,940
想象这个气球是一个恒星
So, imagine the balloon is a star.

150
00:07:52,940 --> 00:07:55,710
这个恒星依靠热核反应保持活力
And the star stays alive by burning thermonuclear fuel,

151
00:07:55,710 --> 00:07:56,780
在它活动的时候
and as it does so,

152
00:07:56,780 --> 00:07:58,810
更重的元素产生，像这海绵
you get heavier elements like the sponge

153
00:07:58,810 --> 00:08:00,780
能量释放
and all that energy released,

154
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:03,320
就像炸弹里的能量释放
like the energy released in a bomb.

155
00:08:03,320 --> 00:08:05,990
当恒星耗尽燃料，它开始变凉
So, as a star runs out of fuel, it begins to cool.

156
00:08:05,990 --> 00:08:10,090
在这过程中，压力下降，无法再支撑自身引力了
And as it cools, it's no longer supported by all that pressure,

157
00:08:10,090 --> 00:08:13,260
于是坍塌开始了
and so it starts to collapse under its own weight.

158
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:16,700
坍塌持续，直到半径缩小到这个程度
And it will continue to collapse until it gets so small

159
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:19,430
物质被压碎
that now you're running up against the essure

160
00:08:19,430 --> 00:08:21,500
挤作一团
of crushing the matter together.

161
00:08:21,500 --> 00:08:22,800
在这个阶段
And at this stage,

162
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:24,740
它的体积比地球稍大一点
it's a little bigger than the size of the Earth,

163
00:08:24,740 --> 00:08:27,370
电子被挤压出来
and it's supported by pushing all of the electrons

164
00:08:27,370 --> 00:08:29,610
原子越来越接近
in the atoms closer and closer together.

165
00:08:29,610 --> 00:08:32,540
如果这个恒星的质量是太阳的几倍
Now, if it's more massive than a couple of times the mass of the sun,

166
00:08:32,550 --> 00:08:34,480
那它就会继续坍塌
it will start to collapse even further.

167
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:37,420
没有任何压力能够阻止这一过程
And there is no form of pressure that can resist this collapse.

168
00:08:37,420 --> 00:08:39,290
坍塌继续，直到
And it will continue to collapse down

169
00:08:39,290 --> 00:08:41,150
恒星变成黑洞
until it forms a black hole.

170
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:48,060
弗里曼:但是，如此怪异的坍塌过程
Freeman: But do such strange crushed corpses of stars

171
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:50,300
真的存在宇宙之中？
really exist out in the cosmos?

172
00:08:52,030 --> 00:08:54,400
它们真的藏身于
Could they be lurking at the center

173
00:08:54,400 --> 00:08:56,970
超新星爆发产生的
of some of those clouds of gas and dust

174
00:08:56,970 --> 00:08:58,640
灰尘和星云之中？
thrown off in a supernova?

175
00:08:58,640 --> 00:09:00,710
[不清楚的谈话声 ]
[ Indistinct conversation ]

176
00:09:00,710 --> 00:09:04,110
Christian Ott 和他的加州理工学院
Christian Ott and his theoretical-astrophysicist group

177
00:09:04,110 --> 00:09:05,780
理论天文研究团队
at caltech

178
00:09:05,780 --> 00:09:07,850
致力于确定爆炸的恒星
are trying to discover whether exploding stars

179
00:09:07,850 --> 00:09:10,620
是否真的会产生黑洞
really do form black holes.

180
00:09:10,620 --> 00:09:12,250
呃，我只是— 你知道
Well, I just generally -- you know,

181
00:09:12,250 --> 00:09:14,590
实际上，我对爆炸的 星星感到十分兴奋
I'm really excited about stars that blow up, actually.

182
00:09:14,590 --> 00:09:16,460
首先，要造成黑洞
First of all, to get a black hole,

183
00:09:16,460 --> 00:09:18,590
你需要很低的，特定的角动量
you need low, specific angular momentum.

184
00:09:18,590 --> 00:09:20,790
要造成急速旋转的黑洞
To have a critically spinning black hole,

185
00:09:20,790 --> 00:09:22,960
就需要很大的角动量，这样
you need a lot of angular momentum, so...

186
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:24,700
弗里曼:有2个办法来确定
Freeman: There are two ways to find out

187
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:27,730
黑洞是否真的由恒星爆炸产生
whether black holes really form when stars blow up.

188
00:09:27,730 --> 00:09:31,970
一个是等，等到我们星系里来一次超新星爆发
One is to wait for a supernova to go off in our galaxy

189
00:09:31,970 --> 00:09:35,570
然后用所有的现代天文学工具研究它
and use every tool of modern astronomy to pick it apart.

190
00:09:35,580 --> 00:09:38,910
Ott:银河系内的超新星爆发会提供大量的信息
Ott: A galactic supernova would provide us so much information,

191
00:09:38,910 --> 00:09:40,850
我们会几周都不睡觉叻。
we wouldn't sleep for weeks.

192
00:09:40,850 --> 00:09:42,780
不过，很不幸
But, unfortunately, it happens

193
00:09:42,780 --> 00:09:46,650
这种事儿每个世纪就来一两次
only maybe once or twice per century.

194
00:09:46,650 --> 00:09:51,060
弗里曼:于是，Christian和他的团队 开始尝试用另一种方法—
Freeman: So, Christian and his team are trying a different approach --

195
00:09:51,060 --> 00:09:54,060
用超级计算机模拟恒星爆炸
blowing up stars inside powerful supercomputers.

196
00:09:54,060 --> 00:09:55,960
简单任务不存在
This is no easy task.

197
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:58,430
实际上，没人能够圆满完成
In fact, no one has pulled it off.

198
00:09:58,430 --> 00:10:02,200
Christian是成功路上第一人
But Christian is on his way to being the first.

199
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:04,700
模拟超新星爆发
So, simulating supernovae stellar collapse

200
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:06,110
和黑洞产生的过程
and black-hole formation

201
00:10:06,110 --> 00:10:09,240
如此困难，因为牵扯到很多 物理学理论
is so hard because it brings together a lot of physics.

202
00:10:09,240 --> 00:10:11,640
和引力有关系
It's general relativity for gravity.

203
00:10:11,640 --> 00:10:14,680
坍塌的气体涉及流动力学
It's fluid dynamics for the gas that collapses.

204
00:10:14,680 --> 00:10:16,480
粒子物理学
It's particle physics.

205
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:17,850
做这种模拟
Doing the simulations,

206
00:10:17,850 --> 00:10:20,190
有点像做一个非常好的天气预报
it's like trying to do a really good weather forecast.

207
00:10:20,190 --> 00:10:22,960
弗里曼:截至目前，Christian进行的一次
Freeman: So far, Christian has failed

208
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:25,160
模拟超新星爆炸的模拟实验
to make a virtual star explode

209
00:10:25,160 --> 00:10:28,390
失败了
in a way that looks like a real supernova.

210
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:31,900
不过在多年的完善物理和数学模型后
But after years of refining the physics and the math,

211
00:10:31,900 --> 00:10:34,330
他现在认为，他可能会是第一个
he now thinks he may be the first

212
00:10:34,330 --> 00:10:37,440
完全明了黑洞诞生过程的人
to fully understand how a black hole is born.

213
00:10:42,980 --> 00:10:47,250
Ott: 伙计，那是一个事件界限
Ott: Man, that is an event horizon right there,

214
00:10:47,250 --> 00:10:48,980
这个黑洞在中间
and this black hole in the center.

215
00:10:48,980 --> 00:10:51,380
哇噢，我们第一次看到
Wow, that's the first time that we do see this.

216
00:10:51,380 --> 00:10:53,150
弗里曼: 令人惊奇的是
Freeman: What's surprising is

217
00:10:53,150 --> 00:10:55,450
最有希望成功的模拟
that the most promising simulations

218
00:10:55,460 --> 00:10:56,920
并不是爆炸
don't actually explode.

219
00:10:56,920 --> 00:10:58,620
只不过是简单的坍塌
They simply collapse.

220
00:10:58,630 --> 00:11:01,560
不是巨大的“砰！”，而是婉约的“嗯~~”
It's not a bang but a whimper.

221
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:07,100
它的名字— 不是超新星，而是暗新星
Its name -- not supernova, but unnova.

222
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:09,230
Ott: 基本上，这就是最终
Ott: It's basically just everything

223
00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:11,400
沉入黑洞的所有东西
eventually sinks into a black hole,

224
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:14,140
缓慢但又命中注定，星球消失了
and the star slowly but surely just disappears.

225
00:11:14,140 --> 00:11:16,180
它可能是真实的，多数恒星
It could be true that most stars,

226
00:11:16,180 --> 00:11:18,780
或者较大的恒星碎片都会消失
or a large fraction of stars, just disappear.

227
00:11:18,780 --> 00:11:22,110
我们没有任何数据 我们从未见过暗新星
We don't have any data on that. We have never seen an unnova.

228
00:11:24,080 --> 00:11:28,250
弗里曼:如果Christian是对的 黑洞很低调，在静默中产生
Freeman: If Christian is right and black holes form silently,

229
00:11:28,250 --> 00:11:31,290
那么，这些太空中的食人族
then these cosmic cannibals could be hidden

230
00:11:31,290 --> 00:11:33,290
也许就藏身我们周围
in plain sight all around us,

231
00:11:33,290 --> 00:11:36,200
面目普通，永不为我们所知
and we might never know it.

232
00:11:36,200 --> 00:11:40,170
寻找黑洞是非常非常困难的
Finding black holes is terribly, terribly difficult.

233
00:11:40,170 --> 00:11:44,240
即使它不是黑的，在辐射能量
Even if it wasn't black and would be radiating energy,

234
00:11:44,240 --> 00:11:47,310
它的直径也只有，大概20英里
it would still be only, let's say, 20 miles across.

235
00:11:47,310 --> 00:11:49,810
即使，你知道，他在10光年之外
And even, you know, at 10 light-years away,

236
00:11:49,810 --> 00:11:51,480
即使是用我们最好的望远镜去找
it would be impossible to find

237
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:53,380
那也是不可能看到的
even with the best telescopes we have.

238
00:11:53,380 --> 00:11:56,850
弗里曼:黑洞极其难以找到
Freeman: But if black holes are almost completely elusive,

239
00:11:56,850 --> 00:11:58,820
却没人告诉他这个事实
no one told this man.

240
00:11:58,820 --> 00:12:03,520
过去30年，他都在全力寻找一个， 一个巨大的
He's spent the past 30 years hunting one, a giant one,

241
00:12:03,520 --> 00:12:06,660
就在我们银河系的心脏部位
right at the heart of our own Milky Way galaxy.

242
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:10,960
他的发现会推翻关于宇宙运行
And his discovery will overturn all our ideas

243
00:12:10,960 --> 00:12:14,370
的现有观念
about how the universe really works.

244
00:12:22,380 --> 00:12:24,380
弗里曼: 1931年
Freeman: In 1931,

245
00:12:24,380 --> 00:12:28,190
一个贝尔电话研究员，Karl Jansky
a bell telephone researcher, Karl Jansky,

246
00:12:28,190 --> 00:12:30,720
在测试一个新系统
was testing a new system for sending radio messages

247
00:12:30,720 --> 00:12:32,590
发射跨大西洋，到欧洲的广播信号
across the Atlantic to Europe.

248
00:12:35,160 --> 00:12:38,230
他要被背景噪声搞死了
He was plagued by background noise.

249
00:12:38,230 --> 00:12:40,700
在2年的细致工作后
After two years of careful work,

250
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:44,740
Jansky 剔除了大部分干扰
Jansky stripped out most of the interference.

251
00:12:44,740 --> 00:12:48,440
但是一个奇怪的信号一直存在
But one strange signal never went away.

252
00:12:48,440 --> 00:12:51,510
只要他的天线指向人马座
It was loudest whenever his antenna was pointed

253
00:12:51,510 --> 00:12:53,510
这个噪声都是最大的
at the constellation Sagittarius

254
00:12:53,510 --> 00:12:56,950
人马座位于银河中心
at the very heart of the Milky Way.

255
00:13:00,050 --> 00:13:04,920
噪声不像是恒星造成的
It was a signal unlike anything a star would make.

256
00:13:04,920 --> 00:13:07,790
天文学家开始猜测噪声来源
Astronomers began to wonder whether it might come

257
00:13:07,790 --> 00:13:11,100
认为那是一个理论上已经预测 但从未被观测的物体—
from an object theorists had predicted but never detected --

258
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:15,600
黑洞
a black hole.

259
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:17,900
可无法证实
But there was no way to find out.

260
00:13:17,900 --> 00:13:20,800
我们银河系的中心
The center of our galaxy is hidden from view

261
00:13:20,810 --> 00:13:23,240
被灰尘面纱所遮掩
by a thick veil of dust.

262
00:13:23,240 --> 00:13:25,740
25年前
Then, 25 years ago,

263
00:13:25,740 --> 00:13:28,610
一个德国天文学家，Reinhard Genzel
a German astronomer, Reinhard Genzel,

264
00:13:28,610 --> 00:13:31,450
发现了透过雾看清花的方法
found a way to see through the fog.

265
00:13:31,450 --> 00:13:34,250
问题关键是我们身处银河
The problem is we are sitting in the Milky Way,

266
00:13:34,250 --> 00:13:37,390
银河像个盘子
and the galactic center is sort of just along the way

267
00:13:37,390 --> 00:13:38,860
我们观察中心
through the entire plane

268
00:13:38,860 --> 00:13:41,460
视线就要穿过几乎整个盘子
of this big spiral galaxy we're sitting in.

269
00:13:41,460 --> 00:13:43,890
在我们和银河中心之间
And there's all this gunk, this dust and this gas,

270
00:13:43,900 --> 00:13:45,730
有这么一大堆灰尘和气体
between us and the galactic center,

271
00:13:45,730 --> 00:13:47,630
所以我们无法在可见光范围观察
so we can't see it in the visible.

272
00:13:47,630 --> 00:13:50,930
但是灰尘对长波的衰减有限
But at longer wavelengths, this dust is not as efficient.

273
00:13:50,940 --> 00:13:54,740
弗里曼:红外线波长较长
Freeman: Infrared light, with its longer wavelength,

274
00:13:54,740 --> 00:13:57,410
很利于穿过面纱
is perfect for penetrating the veil.

275
00:13:57,410 --> 00:13:59,210
但是
But it's terrible

276
00:13:59,210 --> 00:14:02,910
地球大气中的水分 对红外线吸收能力很强
at getting through the water vapor in Earth's atmosphere.

277
00:14:02,910 --> 00:14:04,410
于是Reinhard Genzel跑到了
So Reinhard Genzel headed

278
00:14:04,420 --> 00:14:07,350
地球上最高、最干的地方—
for the highest and driest place on Earth --

279
00:14:07,350 --> 00:14:09,420
智利的阿塔卡马沙漠
the Atacama Desert of Chile.

280
00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:11,590
自1992年开始
Beginning in 1992,

281
00:14:11,590 --> 00:14:14,090
他和他的Max Planck学院团队
he and his team at the Max Planck Institute

282
00:14:14,090 --> 00:14:15,860
开始了
began what would become an enduring campaign

283
00:14:15,860 --> 00:14:19,700
找寻银河中心噪声之源的
to find out exactly what was causing

284
00:14:19,700 --> 00:14:21,870
持久战
the strange noise at the center of the Milky Way.

285
00:14:21,870 --> 00:14:25,200
实际上，我们发现，在银河中心
In fact, we found in the center of the Milky Way

286
00:14:25,200 --> 00:14:27,740
有非常密集的星群
a very dense collection of stars.

287
00:14:27,740 --> 00:14:29,770
那是银河系的中心
That's the very center of the Milky Way,

288
00:14:29,770 --> 00:14:32,010
你知道，星系环绕着它运行
around which, you know, everything turns.

289
00:14:32,010 --> 00:14:33,710
第一个嫌疑人出现—
And then came the first suspicions --

290
00:14:33,710 --> 00:14:35,680
在那里也许有什么东东
maybe there is something there.

291
00:14:35,680 --> 00:14:38,550
弗里曼: Reinhard有种直觉，黑洞
Freeman: Reinhard had a hunch that a black hole

292
00:14:38,550 --> 00:14:41,620
作为一个巨大的引力中心
could be acting as a colossal center of gravity,

293
00:14:41,620 --> 00:14:44,820
会导致群星围绕着它运行
causing dozens of stars to whirl around it.

294
00:14:44,820 --> 00:14:47,660
于是他扎根研究
So he settled in for the long haul.

295
00:14:47,660 --> 00:14:49,860
每年，他拍摄一组照片
Each year, he took another set of pictures,

296
00:14:49,860 --> 00:14:51,600
绘制出银河中心
plotting the movement of that cluster of stars

297
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:54,300
星群运行的轨迹
at our galaxy's heart.

298
00:14:54,300 --> 00:14:55,700
他召集了一个大团队
He gathered a large team

299
00:14:55,700 --> 00:14:58,340
帮助他处理海量的数据
to help him handle the immense amounts of data

300
00:14:58,340 --> 00:15:02,340
并采用了一种叫做 自适应光学的新技术
and used a new technique called adaptive optics

301
00:15:02,340 --> 00:15:07,080
使遥远星球的照片更清晰
to make the images of these distant stars sharper.

302
00:15:07,080 --> 00:15:10,480
如果说，你在普通望远镜中看
So, if you look at what the galactic center would look like

303
00:15:10,480 --> 00:15:12,580
银河系中心
in a normal telescope, let's say,

304
00:15:12,580 --> 00:15:15,120
会得到那样的图像
you would get images which look like that.

305
00:15:15,120 --> 00:15:16,850
自适应光学处理后的效果
The effect of this adaptive optics

306
00:15:16,860 --> 00:15:18,660
就像右手边这样
you can see on the right-hand side.

307
00:15:18,660 --> 00:15:20,860
如此美丽，大让人震惊了
It's just amazing how beautiful that image gets.

308
00:15:20,860 --> 00:15:22,260
同样的美景
It's really the same scene.

309
00:15:22,260 --> 00:15:26,030
你可以识别出左手边 那个模糊的图像上
You can recognize those two stars here on the left-hand side

310
00:15:26,030 --> 00:15:27,560
的2个星星—
in the blurred image there --

311
00:15:27,570 --> 00:15:30,030
右手边图像上的2个星星
these two stars on the right-hand side.

312
00:15:30,030 --> 00:15:33,870
弗里曼:年复一年的研究后， 一个显著的模式显露出来
Freeman: As the years went by, a striking pattern emerged.

313
00:15:33,870 --> 00:15:38,140
星星在运动— 运动的非常快
Stars were moving -- moving really fast.

314
00:15:38,140 --> 00:15:40,380
这是天文学家
This was something that no astronomer

315
00:15:40,380 --> 00:15:41,950
前所未见的景象
had ever seen before --

316
00:15:41,950 --> 00:15:45,050
一打，然后20个，然后30个星星
a dozen, then 20, then 30 stars

317
00:15:45,050 --> 00:15:48,850
都在以极快的速度 环绕着中心某物运行
all swirling at breakneck speed around a central object

318
00:15:48,850 --> 00:15:52,390
完全黑暗，密度极其之大
that was completely dark and tremendously dense.

319
00:15:52,390 --> 00:15:56,360
这会是黑洞存在的证据吗？
Could this be the first proof that black holes existed?

320
00:15:56,360 --> 00:15:59,030
如果是， 那么在银河系的中心
And if so, was there really one here

321
00:15:59,030 --> 00:16:01,670
真的有一个黑洞吗？
right in the center of our own galaxy?

322
00:16:05,200 --> 00:16:08,340
Genzel: 为了看到或者证明
Genzel: What do you do in order to see something

323
00:16:08,340 --> 00:16:10,040
一个你不可能见到的东西
or prove the existence of something

324
00:16:10,040 --> 00:16:12,010
你会怎么做？
which you can't really see, right?

325
00:16:12,010 --> 00:16:14,480
黑洞，你会想，是某物
The black hole, you would think, is something,

326
00:16:14,480 --> 00:16:16,950
被定义为光无法逃脱的物体
well, by definition, light can't escape from.

327
00:16:16,950 --> 00:16:19,650
可你有引力 想想太阳系
But you have gravity. Think of the solar system.

328
00:16:19,650 --> 00:16:21,820
OK，在中心有个太阳
Okay, you have the sun in the center,

329
00:16:21,820 --> 00:16:23,490
行星围绕着它
and then you have the planets.

330
00:16:23,490 --> 00:16:26,360
远日行星的绕日速度很慢
The outer planets move very slowly around the sun.

331
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:29,830
离太阳越近，星星运行速度越快
And the closer you come to the sun, the faster the planets go.

332
00:16:29,830 --> 00:16:32,600
现在，在你的想象中，把太阳换掉
So, suppose in your mind you switch off the sun.

333
00:16:32,600 --> 00:16:35,770
你不得不得出结论 在行星轨道中心有一个物体
You would have to conclude that there is a central object

334
00:16:35,770 --> 00:16:38,500
有着太阳的质量
with one solar mass, around which the planets orbit.

335
00:16:38,500 --> 00:16:40,570
看，这就是我们所做的
See, that's what we're doing.

336
00:16:40,570 --> 00:16:43,570
这样，就有了这些星球
So, these are the stars that are shown.

337
00:16:43,570 --> 00:16:46,810
这儿，在正中心，就是信号源
Here at the very center here is the radio source,

338
00:16:46,810 --> 00:16:50,010
我们怀疑这里有黑洞
which we suspect is the location of the black hole.

339
00:16:50,010 --> 00:16:52,380
这是我们最好的星星
This is our best star,

340
00:16:52,380 --> 00:16:57,190
为了确定完整的轨道， 我们追踪15年了
which we have followed for 15 years to trace a full orbit.

341
00:16:57,190 --> 00:17:00,960
弗里曼: 这颗星，名字是S2
Freeman: This star, known only by the name S2,

342
00:17:00,960 --> 00:17:03,490
运行速度非常惊人
was moving at a phenomenal rate.

343
00:17:03,490 --> 00:17:06,260
在它运行到距离黑暗中心最近的时候
At its closest approach to the dark central object,

344
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:09,100
Reinhard和他的团队替它掐了个表
Reinhard and his team clocked it moving

345
00:17:09,100 --> 00:17:11,800
速度是每小时1100万英里
at 11 million miles per hour.

346
00:17:11,800 --> 00:17:14,570
Genzel: 从这个上面， 我们的研究结果是
Genzel: What we learned from this is that, indeed,

347
00:17:14,570 --> 00:17:16,940
在无线电信号源的位置
there's only one central mass right there

348
00:17:16,940 --> 00:17:19,040
只有一个中心质量
at the position of the radio source,

349
00:17:19,040 --> 00:17:22,350
质量约为4百万个太阳
and that has four million solar masses.

350
00:17:22,350 --> 00:17:25,720
不会有别的可信的形态了
There cannot really be any believable configuration

351
00:17:25,720 --> 00:17:28,190
只有黑洞最可能
which we know of other than the black hole.

352
00:17:31,460 --> 00:17:34,190
弗里曼:Reinhard Genzel做出了黑洞研究史上
Freeman: Reinhard Genzel had made

353
00:17:34,190 --> 00:17:37,830
第一个决定性的发现
the first definitive discovery of a black hole.

354
00:17:37,830 --> 00:17:40,800
不止于此，他的团队还发现了一个星体
But more than that, his team had found an object

355
00:17:40,800 --> 00:17:43,430
在它的一生中，一定吞下了
that must have swallowed millions of stars

356
00:17:43,430 --> 00:17:45,140
上百万个星星
over its lifetime.

357
00:17:45,140 --> 00:17:49,270
天文学家称之为 超级质量黑洞
Astronomers call it a supermassive black hole.

358
00:17:49,270 --> 00:17:53,040
这个发现意义重大
But despite the enormity of this discovery,

359
00:17:53,040 --> 00:17:54,380
但这也许只是一系列
it would be just the first

360
00:17:54,380 --> 00:17:58,350
怪异和令人不安的发现中的第一个
of many increasingly bizarre and disturbing findings.

361
00:17:58,350 --> 00:18:00,920
接下来要弄清楚
The next was to figure out

362
00:18:00,920 --> 00:18:03,290
黑洞中发生着什么事
what goes on inside a black hole.

363
00:18:03,290 --> 00:18:07,020
如果恒星、行星，甚至是人
What happens to stars, planets, even people

364
00:18:07,030 --> 00:18:10,260
离这个宇宙下水道太近， 会发生什么？
if they get too close to this cosmic sinkhole?

365
00:18:10,260 --> 00:18:14,930
没有望远镜能够看到黑洞里面
No telescope can ever see inside black holes.

366
00:18:14,930 --> 00:18:17,600
为了知道它们如何扭曲现实
To understand how they twist reality,

367
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:21,570
我们要停止看，学会听
we have to stop looking and learn how to listen.

368
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:29,970
在我们银河的中心
Lurking at the center of our galaxy

369
00:18:29,970 --> 00:18:32,610
潜伏着一个完全不可见
is an object that's completely invisible

370
00:18:32,610 --> 00:18:36,610
但却有四百万个太阳之重的物体
but weighs as much as four million stars.

371
00:18:36,610 --> 00:18:39,880
天文学家现在相信，几乎每个星系中心
Astronomers now believe almost every galaxy has

372
00:18:39,880 --> 00:18:43,220
都有一个超质量黑洞
a supermassive black hole at its core.

373
00:18:43,220 --> 00:18:45,980
那么，它们是什么？
So, what are they?

374
00:18:46,580 --> 00:18:50,680
科幻小说视黑洞为 宇宙时间机器
Science fiction sees black holes as cosmic time machines

375
00:18:50,980 --> 00:18:53,460
或者通往平行宇宙之门
or portals to a parallel universe.

376
00:18:55,480 --> 00:18:56,390
可是，真正的科学家发现
But real scientists are finding

377
00:18:56,400 --> 00:19:00,370
事实要比科幻小说怪的多
that truth is stranger than sci fi.

378
00:19:00,370 --> 00:19:02,670
你会进入一个世界
You're about to enter a world

379
00:19:02,670 --> 00:19:05,640
这里极大即极小
where the very big and the very small

380
00:19:05,640 --> 00:19:07,540
极小即极大
are indistinguishable,

381
00:19:07,540 --> 00:19:12,840
这里现实和幻想等价
where reality and illusion are one and the same.

382
00:19:12,850 --> 00:19:15,310
天文学家Julie Comerford
Astronomer Julie Comerford

383
00:19:15,310 --> 00:19:18,850
研究了几十个遥远星系的中心
has been studying the centers of dozens of distant galaxies,

384
00:19:18,850 --> 00:19:22,320
试图找到黑洞的迹象
trying to find signs of black holes,

385
00:19:22,320 --> 00:19:27,260
希望多了解这些费解的物体
hoping to learn more about these mind-bending objects.

386
00:19:27,260 --> 00:19:29,460
事实证明，在所有或者几乎所有的星系
It turns out that in all or nearly all galaxies,

387
00:19:29,460 --> 00:19:30,830
我们目之所及
wherever we look,

388
00:19:30,830 --> 00:19:32,960
星系中心有一个超质量黑洞
they have a central supermassive black hole at their heart.

389
00:19:32,970 --> 00:19:34,930
超质量黑洞
Supermassive ones are the ones that have masses

390
00:19:34,930 --> 00:19:36,840
其质量是太阳的
of anywhere from a million to a billion times

391
00:19:36,840 --> 00:19:38,570
十万到百万倍
the mass of the sun.

392
00:19:38,570 --> 00:19:40,410
当气体落到超质量黑洞上时
You can see a supermassive black hole

393
00:19:40,410 --> 00:19:41,670
你能看到它
when gas is falling onto it.

394
00:19:41,670 --> 00:19:43,810
在气体落在它上面那一刻
And sort of right before the gas falls into it,

395
00:19:43,810 --> 00:19:46,580
它会加热，释放出大量能量
it gets heated up and emits a lot of energy

396
00:19:46,580 --> 00:19:49,280
会变得非常亮
and can appear really bright.

397
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:52,250
弗里曼: 但是当Julie研究这些环绕在
Freeman: But when Julie investigates the glowing gas

398
00:19:52,250 --> 00:19:54,990
巨大黑洞周围的发亮气体时
surrounding these giant black holes,

399
00:19:54,990 --> 00:19:58,090
想象不到的事发生了
she finds something totally unexpected.

400
00:20:00,860 --> 00:20:03,530
在无法想象的尺度上
There's a cosmic dance going on

401
00:20:03,530 --> 00:20:05,870
宇宙舞蹈在持续不停
on a scale that's almost unimaginable.

402
00:20:07,870 --> 00:20:10,940
你看到了2个光峰，而不是1个
You saw two peaks in the light instead of just one.

403
00:20:10,940 --> 00:20:12,640
你期望从星系中的黑洞
You'd expect one from one black hole

404
00:20:12,640 --> 00:20:14,770
观测到1个光峰
that's just sitting at rest in its galaxy,

405
00:20:14,770 --> 00:20:17,210
可我们却看到2个， 速度还不同
but we saw two peaks with different velocities.

406
00:20:17,210 --> 00:20:18,640
这立即引起我们兴趣
And that immediately hit us,

407
00:20:18,640 --> 00:20:20,850
这一定是有意思的事情
as this has got to be something interesting.

408
00:20:20,850 --> 00:20:22,610
弗里曼:Julie在思考
Freeman: Julie began thinking

409
00:20:22,620 --> 00:20:25,720
如果两个星系碰撞会怎样
about what would happen when two galaxies collide.

410
00:20:25,720 --> 00:20:28,590
如果它们中心都有黑洞又如何？
And if both had black holes at their centers,

411
00:20:28,590 --> 00:20:32,090
这些超重小老头儿会发生什么事？
what would happen to those massive objects?

412
00:20:32,090 --> 00:20:33,860
Comerford:当两个星系碰撞
Comerford: So, when two galaxies collide,

413
00:20:33,860 --> 00:20:35,360
它们中心的黑洞
the black holes at their center --

414
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:36,630
不会迎头碰撞
instead of crashing in head-on,

415
00:20:36,630 --> 00:20:39,500
它们开始旋转，开始跳舞
they begin this swirl, or dance.

416
00:20:39,500 --> 00:20:43,070
我们观测这些跳华尔兹的黑洞的方法
And the way that we can detect these waltzing black holes

417
00:20:43,070 --> 00:20:45,270
是观测射出的光
is by looking at the light that's emitted from them.

418
00:20:45,270 --> 00:20:48,010
对于向我们移动的黑洞
So, for the black hole that's moving towards us,

419
00:20:48,010 --> 00:20:50,880
我们探测到较短波长的光
we detect light that is at smaller wavelengths,

420
00:20:50,880 --> 00:20:53,310
压缩在一起，我们看到蓝移的光
scrunched up together, so we see bluer light.

421
00:20:53,310 --> 00:20:55,680
对于正在远离我们的黑洞
And for the black hole that's moving away from us,

422
00:20:55,680 --> 00:20:58,080
我们看到拉开来，波长较长的光
we see stretched-out, longer-wavelength light

423
00:20:58,080 --> 00:20:59,850
红移的光
that appears redder.

424
00:20:59,850 --> 00:21:01,920
偏红和偏蓝的光
So it's this redder and bluer light

425
00:21:01,920 --> 00:21:04,720
是黑洞华尔兹的标志
that is a telltale signature of a black-hole waltz.

426
00:21:04,720 --> 00:21:05,960
每次我们看到这些光
Every time we see it,

427
00:21:05,960 --> 00:21:08,090
我们就在观测室里击掌相庆
we high-five in the observation room,

428
00:21:08,090 --> 00:21:09,790
你无法克制
and you just can't get over it.

429
00:21:09,800 --> 00:21:12,700
弗里曼:Julie 扫描了宇宙
Freeman: As Julie scans the universe,

430
00:21:12,700 --> 00:21:14,530
她发现同样的标志性舞蹈
she finds the same remarkable dance

431
00:21:14,530 --> 00:21:17,000
在一个又一个星系
happening time and time again.

432
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
一次次的重复上演
In galaxy after galaxy,

433
00:21:19,010 --> 00:21:20,940
黑洞出双入对
black holes are paired up

434
00:21:20,940 --> 00:21:23,980
激情共舞
and dancing the cosmic night away.

435
00:21:23,980 --> 00:21:26,010
我们研究了90个星系
So, we identified 90 galaxies

436
00:21:26,010 --> 00:21:29,050
最老的有宇宙的一半年纪
from when the universe was half its present age,

437
00:21:29,050 --> 00:21:32,080
我们发现，32个，约三分之一
and we found that fully 32 of them, or about a third,

438
00:21:32,080 --> 00:21:35,350
有红-蓝标志，成对的黑洞
had black holes that exhibited this blue-and-red signature.

439
00:21:35,350 --> 00:21:36,990
我们很惊讶
So that was really surprising

440
00:21:36,990 --> 00:21:39,560
如此高比例的黑洞
that such a high fraction of the black holes

441
00:21:39,560 --> 00:21:42,330
并不是待在星系中心静止不动
were not stationary at the center of the galaxy at all,

442
00:21:42,330 --> 00:21:45,330
而是在和别的黑洞跳华尔兹
that they were undergoing this waltz with another black hole.

443
00:21:48,070 --> 00:21:50,470
弗里曼: Janna Levin 这样的科学家相信
Freeman: Scientists like Janna Levin believe

444
00:21:50,470 --> 00:21:52,540
华尔兹黑洞的发现
the discovery of waltzing black holes

445
00:21:52,540 --> 00:21:55,570
开创了一个研究它们内部 的新方法
opens up a whole new way to learn what's inside them,

446
00:21:55,570 --> 00:21:58,710
因为它们的舞姿也许不仅是可见的
because their dance might not only be visible.

447
00:21:58,710 --> 00:22:01,580
舞姿也许是可听的
It could also be audible.

448
00:22:01,580 --> 00:22:05,250
科学前瞻者，爱因斯坦
The scientific visionary Albert Einstein

449
00:22:05,250 --> 00:22:08,150
发现时间和空间是有弹性的材料
saw space and time as a flexible material

450
00:22:08,150 --> 00:22:10,690
能够被引力扭曲
that could be distorted by gravity.

451
00:22:10,690 --> 00:22:14,860
黑洞是材料上的 一个非常深的井
A black hole is merely a very deep well in this material.

452
00:22:14,860 --> 00:22:18,400
当两个黑洞彼此靠近
When two black holes come close to one another,

453
00:22:18,400 --> 00:22:20,230
这两个旋转的井
these two orbiting wells

454
00:22:20,230 --> 00:22:23,540
搅拌时空 散发出波
stir up space-time and send out ripples

455
00:22:23,540 --> 00:22:25,750
清晰传播
that can travel clear across the universe.

456
00:22:25,850 --> 00:22:28,750
这些波以光速旅行
And these waves will move out through the universe,

457
00:22:28,760 --> 00:22:30,560
穿越整个宇宙
traveling at the speed of light.

458
00:22:30,560 --> 00:22:33,330
我们虽然不能看到黑洞
So we can hope to not see black holes with light

459
00:22:33,330 --> 00:22:35,460
但也许，换种方法，听它们
but maybe, in some sense, hear them

460
00:22:35,460 --> 00:22:37,260
如果我们能够接收到
if we can pick up the wobbling

461
00:22:37,260 --> 00:22:39,570
黑洞本身的摆动
of the fabric of space-time itself.

462
00:22:39,570 --> 00:22:42,840
弗里曼:过去几年里
Freeman: For the past several years,

463
00:22:42,840 --> 00:22:45,540
Janna 和她的同事试图预测
Janna and her colleagues have been trying to predict

464
00:22:45,540 --> 00:22:49,640
黑洞彼此环绕时发出的声音
the sounds black holes make as they spin around one another.

465
00:22:49,640 --> 00:22:52,680
计算不是胆小鬼能胜任的
The calculations are not for the faint of heart.

466
00:22:52,680 --> 00:22:55,850
宇宙中两个巨无霸相遇， 在时空之海中掀起风暴
Modeling what happens when two giant objects

467
00:22:55,850 --> 00:22:58,380
要建模描述这样复杂的情况
create a storm in the sea of space-time

468
00:22:58,390 --> 00:23:03,690
需要高深的数学 和几个月的超级运算
takes some serious math and months of supercomputing.

469
00:23:03,690 --> 00:23:05,930
Levin:这是一个小黑洞的轨道
Levin: This is the orbit of a small black hole

470
00:23:05,930 --> 00:23:07,530
围绕着一个大黑洞
around a bigger black hole,

471
00:23:07,530 --> 00:23:10,730
它的声音和敲鼓差不多
and it's literally making a knocking sound on the drum,

472
00:23:10,730 --> 00:23:12,730
鼓就是时空本身
where the drum is space-time itself.

473
00:23:12,730 --> 00:23:15,670
呃，它实际上听上去像— 像是敲击声
Well, it really sounds like -- sounds like a knocking.

474
00:23:15,670 --> 00:23:19,140
当它飞近大黑洞
It starts to get a higher frequency and happen faster,

475
00:23:19,140 --> 00:23:22,210
频率会变高
until it falls into the big black hole

476
00:23:22,210 --> 00:23:23,840
声音加快
and goes down the throat.

477
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:25,980
两个黑洞转作一团
And then the two will ring out together

478
00:23:25,980 --> 00:23:28,950
合二为一
and form one black hole at the end of the day.

479
00:23:28,950 --> 00:23:31,850
这时，你知道，“啵”的一声
And then it just sort of, you know, "brr," chirps up.

480
00:23:31,850 --> 00:23:33,620
弗里曼: 由于黑洞
Freeman: Because black holes

481
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:36,590
对它们周围的时空 影响如此之大
stir up the space and time around them so much,

482
00:23:36,590 --> 00:23:39,690
一个黑洞环绕另一个的轨道
the orbit of one black hole around another

483
00:23:39,690 --> 00:23:43,460
和地球绕日的轨道 看上去完全不同
looks nothing like the orbit of Earth around the sun.

484
00:23:43,460 --> 00:23:45,870
一个轨道滑向黑洞
An orbit can come in around a black hole

485
00:23:45,870 --> 00:23:48,330
在它移动走以前
and do an entire circle as it loops around

486
00:23:48,340 --> 00:23:49,870
环绕一整圈
before it moves out again.

487
00:23:49,870 --> 00:23:51,570
所以，并没有圆形轨道
So instead of getting an oval,

488
00:23:51,570 --> 00:23:54,440
而是一个像三叶苜蓿的形状
you get a three-leaf clover that processes around.

489
00:23:54,440 --> 00:23:56,580
弗里曼:三叶草模式
Freeman: This cloverleaf pattern

490
00:23:56,580 --> 00:23:58,910
在模拟中持续出现
keeps coming out of the simulations.

491
00:23:58,910 --> 00:24:02,520
Janna很震惊
Janna was shocked because this picture

492
00:24:02,520 --> 00:24:04,380
因为两个宇宙中质量最大的物体
of how two of the heaviest objects in the universe

493
00:24:04,390 --> 00:24:06,590
彼此环绕的运行
move around one another

494
00:24:06,590 --> 00:24:09,120
看上去和两种质量极小物体
bears an uncanny resemblance to the way

495
00:24:09,120 --> 00:24:12,390
的环绕运行极为相似
two of the lightest objects move around one another --

496
00:24:12,390 --> 00:24:17,030
就像原子里质子和电子的运动模式
the tiny protons and electrons inside an atom.

497
00:24:17,030 --> 00:24:19,200
我们可以建立一个模型，像原子
We can build a kind of classical atom

498
00:24:19,200 --> 00:24:21,500
一个大黑洞，像原子核
out of a big black hole, like a nucleus,

499
00:24:21,500 --> 00:24:24,370
一个小黑洞，像电子
and a light black hole, which acts like an electron.

500
00:24:24,370 --> 00:24:28,140
某种意义上，它们共同形成一个原子
And together, they form a real atom, in a sense.

501
00:24:28,140 --> 00:24:32,510
弗里曼: 质量如此之大的 一个物体怎么能
Freeman: How could an object that weighs so much

502
00:24:32,510 --> 00:24:37,450
像一个亚原子粒子那样的 小东西一样运行
behave like a subatomic particle that weighs so little?

503
00:24:37,450 --> 00:24:40,790
所谓的普通物体
When we talk about ordinary objects, or people even,

504
00:24:40,790 --> 00:24:42,790
它们之间并不完全相同
they are never exactly the same.

505
00:24:42,790 --> 00:24:44,520
我的意思是，你可以试图 克隆一个我
I mean, you could try to clone me,

506
00:24:44,520 --> 00:24:46,330
但也是一个不同的复制品
and still the different copies of me

507
00:24:46,330 --> 00:24:48,160
不会完全相同
would not be exactly the same.

508
00:24:48,160 --> 00:24:50,860
在这个意义上， 人和普通物体
In that sense, people and ordinary objects

509
00:24:50,860 --> 00:24:53,170
不像基本粒子
are not like fundamental particles.

510
00:24:53,170 --> 00:24:54,700
它们是可辨别的
They're distinguishable.

511
00:24:54,700 --> 00:24:57,000
而黑洞却非常不同
But the black hole is quite different from that.

512
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,110
黑洞像是基本粒子
Black holes are like fundamental particles,

513
00:24:59,110 --> 00:25:00,370
这令人吃惊
and that's very surprising

514
00:25:00,370 --> 00:25:02,440
因为黑洞是巨大的，宏观的物体
because they're huge, macroscopic objects.

515
00:25:02,440 --> 00:25:07,180
弗里曼: 现在，这个想法 只是诱人的直觉
Freeman: Right now, this idea is only a tantalizing hunch.

516
00:25:07,180 --> 00:25:10,480
不过5年内，超灵敏探测器
But in just five years, super-sensitive detectors

517
00:25:10,480 --> 00:25:13,490
将有能力探测到黑洞互相环绕时
should be able to pick up the ripples in space created

518
00:25:13,490 --> 00:25:16,920
放射出的波信号
by two massive black holes spinning around one another.

519
00:25:16,920 --> 00:25:18,820
它们会告诉我们
And they'll tell us

520
00:25:18,830 --> 00:25:22,930
黑洞是否真的像原子一样运动
whether they really do behave like tiny atoms.

521
00:25:22,930 --> 00:25:26,470
但是这种极大和极小之间的联系
But this connection between the very big and the very small

522
00:25:26,470 --> 00:25:28,030
已经点燃了两位在世的
has already sparked a war

523
00:25:28,040 --> 00:25:30,870
最伟大物理学家之间的战火
between two of the greatest living physicists.

524
00:25:33,010 --> 00:25:35,210
一位是—史蒂芬 霍金
One of them -- Stephen Hawking.

525
00:25:35,210 --> 00:25:39,580
另一位，本来是南Bronx的 一个超级玛丽，水管工
The other began life as a plumber in the South Bronx

526
00:25:39,580 --> 00:25:42,650
现在他用黑洞发展了
and is now using black holes to develop

527
00:25:42,650 --> 00:25:47,850
自爱因斯坦以来 最具革命性的物理思想
the most revolutionary idea in physics since Albert Einstein --

528
00:25:47,850 --> 00:25:52,090
这个思想把现实搞得天翻地覆
an idea that literally turns reality inside out.

529
00:25:55,760 --> 00:25:58,330
弗里曼:联合宏观和微观的
Freeman: The first step in joining

530
00:25:58,330 --> 00:26:01,730
第一步始于1974
the physics of the very large and the very small

531
00:26:01,730 --> 00:26:06,970
在霍金的大脑里
came in 1974 from the mind of Stephen Hawking.

532
00:26:06,970 --> 00:26:10,710
关于微观的理论，量子力学
The theory of the very small, quantum mechanics,

533
00:26:10,710 --> 00:26:13,680
预言空间中存在
predicts that empty space should be sizzling

534
00:26:13,680 --> 00:26:16,140
粒子和反粒子
with particles and antiparticles,

535
00:26:16,150 --> 00:26:18,310
成双成对
popping into existence in pairs

536
00:26:18,310 --> 00:26:21,950
然后立即合并，湮灭无踪
and then annihilating one another an instant later.

537
00:26:21,950 --> 00:26:25,120
这些粒子存在时间如此之短
These particles exist for such a short time,

538
00:26:25,120 --> 00:26:27,660
它们并不被认为是真实的一部分
they're not considered part of reality.

539
00:26:27,660 --> 00:26:30,730
物理学家称之为虚拟粒子
Physicists call them virtual particles.

540
00:26:30,730 --> 00:26:32,330
但是霍金认识到
But Hawking realized

541
00:26:32,330 --> 00:26:35,630
在宇宙中有特别的一隅
there was one special place in the universe

542
00:26:35,630 --> 00:26:38,730
这些粒子会变为真实
where these particles could become real.

543
00:26:38,740 --> 00:26:39,800
在黑洞附近
Around a black hole,

544
00:26:39,800 --> 00:26:41,670
空间中有一道看不见的界限
there is an invisible line in space

545
00:26:41,670 --> 00:26:43,910
叫做事件界限
called the event horizon.

546
00:26:43,910 --> 00:26:45,640
在界限之外
Outside that line,

547
00:26:45,640 --> 00:26:49,410
黑洞的引力如此之弱，无法约束光线
the hole's gravity is just too weak to trap light.

548
00:26:49,410 --> 00:26:52,380
界限之内，没有东西能逃脱它的引力
Inside it, nothing can escape its pull.

549
00:26:52,380 --> 00:26:55,250
如果一对虚拟粒子恰巧出现在
If a pair of virtual particles fmed

550
00:26:55,250 --> 00:26:57,490
事件界限之外
just outside the event horizon,

551
00:26:57,490 --> 00:26:58,820
这对粒子中的一个
then one of the pair

552
00:26:58,820 --> 00:27:01,620
也许会在湮灭之前
might travel across that point of no return

553
00:27:01,620 --> 00:27:04,330
穿越界限
before being able to recombine,

554
00:27:04,330 --> 00:27:07,200
落向黑洞，把它的那一半落在界限外
falling into the black hole and leaving its partner

555
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:12,000
作为真实的辐射放出 ——这辐射称为霍金辐射
to escape as real radiation -- Hawking radiation.

556
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,400
如果霍金是对的
If Hawking is right,

557
00:27:14,400 --> 00:27:17,670
黑洞也许不是全黑的
black holes should not actually be black.

558
00:27:17,670 --> 00:27:21,010
它们也许会微微的闪光
They should shine ever so faintly.

559
00:27:21,010 --> 00:27:24,950
还没有人能从黑洞的边缘
No one has ever detected Hawking radiation

560
00:27:24,950 --> 00:27:27,250
探测到霍金辐射
from the rim of a black hole.

561
00:27:27,250 --> 00:27:31,390
实际上，辐射如此之弱 黑洞如此之远
In fact, it's so faint, and black holes are so far away,

562
00:27:31,390 --> 00:27:33,990
探测也许是永远不可能的任务
that it will probably never be possible.

563
00:27:33,990 --> 00:27:36,590
但是Jeff Steinhauer 认为他能够找到一个办法
But Jeff Steinhauer thinks he's found a way

564
00:27:36,590 --> 00:27:38,130
来测试霍金的理论
to test Hawking's theory

565
00:27:38,130 --> 00:27:42,660
给物理学世界带来震荡
and send shock waves through the world of physics.

566
00:27:42,670 --> 00:27:46,370
他是这个星球上唯一的
He's the only person on the planet

567
00:27:46,370 --> 00:27:48,670
仔细观察过黑洞的人
who has seen a black hole from up close.

568
00:27:48,670 --> 00:27:51,970
实际上，他学会如何制造一个
In fact, he's learned how to create one.

569
00:27:51,980 --> 00:27:54,640
我的黑洞是乖宝宝，一点不危险
My black hole is in no way dangerous.

570
00:27:54,640 --> 00:27:58,510
它是个超声波黑洞， 只吸收超声波
It's a sonic black hole that can only absorb sound waves.

571
00:27:58,510 --> 00:28:02,150
它由十万个原子组成 只是很小的质量
It's only made of 100,000 atoms, which is very little matter.

572
00:28:02,150 --> 00:28:04,390
我肯定，我的邻居会喜欢
And I'm sure that my neighbors would love

573
00:28:04,390 --> 00:28:07,520
我在公寓旁边放一个超声波黑洞的
that I would put a sonic black hole around my apartment,

574
00:28:07,520 --> 00:28:10,660
但我不会这样干滴
but it's not gonna happen.

575
00:28:10,660 --> 00:28:13,800
弗里曼: 如果他没在以色列海法大学物理系的
Freeman: When he's not jamming in the basement

576
00:28:13,800 --> 00:28:16,870
地下室里打鼓取乐
of the physics department at the Technion in Israel,

577
00:28:16,870 --> 00:28:19,600
那他就在楼上的实验室里
he's upstairs in his lab.

578
00:28:19,600 --> 00:28:22,900
Jeff Steinhauer制造超声黑洞的秘诀
Jeff Steinhauer's recipe for making a sonic black hole

579
00:28:22,910 --> 00:28:25,870
起始于一个冷冻到华氏零下459度的
begins with a tiny sample of rubidium atoms

580
00:28:25,880 --> 00:28:30,650
微小铷原子样本
chilled down to minus-459 degrees fahrenheit.

581
00:28:30,650 --> 00:28:33,620
当我在捣鼓这些超低温的原子时
While I was working with these very cold atoms,

582
00:28:33,620 --> 00:28:35,420
我偶然观察到一个现象
I stumbled across a phenomenon.

583
00:28:35,420 --> 00:28:38,190
当原子们以超音速的
When the atoms are actually flowing

584
00:28:38,190 --> 00:28:39,790
速度流动时
faster than the speed of sound,

585
00:28:39,790 --> 00:28:41,560
如果声波试图
then, if there are sound waves

586
00:28:41,560 --> 00:28:43,430
逆原子流而上
trying to travel against the flow,

587
00:28:43,430 --> 00:28:44,630
它们是无法前进的
they can't go forward.

588
00:28:44,630 --> 00:28:47,060
这模拟了黑洞现象
And this is analogous to a real black hole,

589
00:28:47,060 --> 00:28:50,370
黑洞里是光线无法逃脱强大的引力
where light waves cannot escape due to the strong gravitation.

590
00:28:51,900 --> 00:28:54,000
弗里曼:虽然这个黑洞
Freeman: Even though this black hole

591
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,270
只能抓住声波，而不是光线
traps only sound, not light,

592
00:28:56,270 --> 00:28:59,510
但仍满足量子力学的原理
the same laws of quantum mechanics apply to it

593
00:28:59,510 --> 00:29:01,940
就像它们在宇宙中的表兄一样
as they do to its cosmic cousins.

594
00:29:01,940 --> 00:29:05,750
如果霍金关于黑洞的理论是正确的
If Hawking's theory about black holes is correct,

595
00:29:05,750 --> 00:29:09,280
Jeff 应该能够探测到微弱的声波泄漏
Jeff should be able to detect tiny sound waves escaping.

596
00:29:09,290 --> 00:29:11,890
Steinhauer: 应该存在成对的声波
Steinhauer: There should be pairs of sound waves,

597
00:29:11,890 --> 00:29:13,990
左边一个，右边一个
one on the right side and one on the left side.

598
00:29:13,990 --> 00:29:18,030
根据量子力学，它们会突然出现
Due to the quantum physics, they will suddenly be created.

599
00:29:18,030 --> 00:29:21,630
存在难寻的霍金辐射
This is the elusive Hawking radiation.

600
00:29:21,630 --> 00:29:25,670
弗里曼: Jeff还没有找到这种难寻的辐射
Freeman: Jeff has not detected this elusive radiation yet.

601
00:29:25,670 --> 00:29:28,540
但他相信他能在一年内找到
But he believes he should within a year

602
00:29:28,540 --> 00:29:31,340
因为他改进了试验
as he refines his experiment.

603
00:29:31,340 --> 00:29:33,880
没人比 Leonard Susskind更焦急的
No one will await that news

604
00:29:33,880 --> 00:29:37,250
等待试验结果
more keenly than Leonard Susskind.

605
00:29:37,250 --> 00:29:40,150
过去30年的大部分时间里
He has spent much of the last 30 years

606
00:29:40,150 --> 00:29:42,580
他都在考虑霍金辐射的问题
thinking about Hawking radiation

607
00:29:42,590 --> 00:29:46,590
并被其含义深深困扰
and being deeply troubled by what it means.

608
00:29:46,590 --> 00:29:50,890
今天，他是世界上 顶尖理论物理学家之一
Today, he is one of the world's leading theoretical physicists.

609
00:29:50,890 --> 00:29:54,100
但这地位可不是天生的
But that's not the way he started.

610
00:29:54,100 --> 00:29:56,260
当我16岁时，我是个管子工
When I was 16 years old, I was a plumber.

611
00:29:56,270 --> 00:30:00,640
在南Bronx的出租公寓里
Fixing toilets and sewers and so forth

612
00:30:00,640 --> 00:30:03,270
修理马桶和下水道
in tenement buildings in the South Bronx

613
00:30:03,270 --> 00:30:05,840
我可不想就这样消磨掉下半生
was not what I wanted to be doing for the rest of my life.

614
00:30:05,840 --> 00:30:09,240
当我做物理类比时
Whenever I make analogies about physics,

615
00:30:09,250 --> 00:30:12,480
我总感觉和管子有关系
it always seems that they have something to do with plumbing.

616
00:30:12,480 --> 00:30:15,450
我用于研究黑洞的类比
The analogy that I've used over and over about black holes

617
00:30:15,450 --> 00:30:18,890
就像水流入下水道
is water going down a drain.

618
00:30:18,890 --> 00:30:20,360
弦理论的发明
The invention of string theory,

619
00:30:20,360 --> 00:30:22,990
和管子很有渊源
which has a lot to do with tubes --

620
00:30:22,990 --> 00:30:24,130
某些人甚至说
some people even say

621
00:30:24,130 --> 00:30:26,330
因为Susskind是个管子工
this must've been Susskind the plumber.

622
00:30:26,330 --> 00:30:30,500
弗里曼: Leonard Susskind对黑洞的痴迷
Freeman: Leonard Susskind's fascination with black holes

623
00:30:30,500 --> 00:30:32,470
始于三十年前
began 30 years ago

624
00:30:32,470 --> 00:30:35,600
当他听到一个霍金的演讲
when he listened to a talk by Stephen Hawking --

625
00:30:35,610 --> 00:30:39,110
这个演讲激发了强烈的反应
a talk that triggered a violent reaction.

626
00:30:39,110 --> 00:30:41,910
我第一次听到霍金的讲座
I first heard Stephen Hawking give a lecture

627
00:30:41,910 --> 00:30:43,980
是在旧金山
up in San Francisco,

628
00:30:43,980 --> 00:30:47,320
在讲座上，他断言
in which he made this extraordinary claim

629
00:30:47,320 --> 00:30:50,450
黑洞看上去违反
that black holes seem to violate

630
00:30:50,450 --> 00:30:53,390
非常非常基础的物理定律
the very, very fundamental principle of physics

631
00:30:53,390 --> 00:30:56,160
即信息保持
called conservation of information.

632
00:30:56,160 --> 00:30:59,190
弗里曼: 霍金在做出关于黑洞辐射的开创性
Freeman: Seven years after his groundbreaking work

633
00:30:59,200 --> 00:31:01,160
工作七年之后
on black-hole radiation,

634
00:31:01,160 --> 00:31:05,370
他根据这一思想做出逻辑推论
Hawking had taken the idea to its logical conclusion.

635
00:31:05,370 --> 00:31:07,400
对于黑洞吸收的
For every ounce of material

636
00:31:07,400 --> 00:31:10,270
每一盎司质量而言
a black hole absorbed into its core,

637
00:31:10,270 --> 00:31:14,140
它会从事件界限辐射出
it would radiate away an equivalent amount of energy

638
00:31:14,140 --> 00:31:16,040
相当量的能量
from its event horizon.

639
00:31:16,050 --> 00:31:18,080
但是由于在黑洞的事件界限
But since there is no physical link

640
00:31:18,080 --> 00:31:21,120
和中心之间并不存在物理联系
between the center of a black hole and its event horizon,

641
00:31:21,120 --> 00:31:25,550
这两个进程不会分享任何信息
the two processes could not share any information.

642
00:31:25,560 --> 00:31:28,460
现在，从这一物理学 基础定律的角度看
Now, this was a disaster from the point of view

643
00:31:28,460 --> 00:31:30,730
这是一场灾难
of the basic principles of physics.

644
00:31:30,730 --> 00:31:32,990
这一物理学基本定律说
The basic principles of physics say

645
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:34,860
你不可能丢失信息
that you can't lose information.

646
00:31:34,860 --> 00:31:37,730
我给你个例子
Let me give you an exale.

647
00:31:37,730 --> 00:31:39,900
这是一个水槽
Here's a sink of water.

648
00:31:39,900 --> 00:31:43,100
假设给这盆水发送个信息
Imagine sending in a message into that sink of water

649
00:31:43,110 --> 00:31:46,470
以滴红墨水的方式， 用摩尔斯电码编码
in the form of morse code by dropping in this red ink.

650
00:31:46,480 --> 00:31:50,080
漏，漏，漏，滴，漏
Drip, drip, drip, drop, drip.

651
00:31:50,080 --> 00:31:52,410
你看到红墨水旋转
You see the red ink swirling around,

652
00:31:52,420 --> 00:31:55,250
但是如果等上几个小时，
but if you wait a few hours, what will happen

653
00:31:55,250 --> 00:31:58,820
红墨水就会散布水中
is that red ink will get diffused throughout the water.

654
00:31:58,820 --> 00:32:01,160
你会说 恩，额滴神呀
You might say, well, my goodness,

655
00:32:01,160 --> 00:32:02,620
很明显，信息丢失了—
the information is clearly lost --

656
00:32:02,630 --> 00:32:05,230
没人能够重建这信息了
nobody can reconstruct it now.

657
00:32:05,230 --> 00:32:08,960
但是根据非常基本的物理原理
But down at the very core of physical principles,

658
00:32:08,970 --> 00:32:11,100
不，信息还在
no, that information is there.

659
00:32:11,100 --> 00:32:14,970
如果你能看到每个单一分子
If you could watch every single molecule,

660
00:32:14,970 --> 00:32:17,140
你就能够重建那条消息
you could reconstruct that message.

661
00:32:17,140 --> 00:32:19,840
对人类来说这很困难
It may be much too hard for human beings

662
00:32:19,840 --> 00:32:23,750
人们无法重建和跟踪这些运动
to be able to reconstruct and to follow all those motions,

663
00:32:23,750 --> 00:32:26,350
但是物理学家说，消息就在那儿
but physics says it's there.

664
00:32:26,350 --> 00:32:29,450
弗里曼:但是霍金声称
Freeman: But Stephen Hawking claimed

665
00:32:29,450 --> 00:32:32,820
宇宙中的某些特殊场所
there are special places in the universe

666
00:32:32,820 --> 00:32:35,560
这一定律会被打破
where that law can be broken.

667
00:32:35,560 --> 00:32:38,160
Susskind: 当信息进入黑洞时
Susskind: What happens when the information

668
00:32:38,160 --> 00:32:40,030
发生了什么？
goes down the black hole?

669
00:32:40,030 --> 00:32:42,860
对霍金来说
The answer, according to Stephen,

670
00:32:42,870 --> 00:32:44,930
信息流入下水道
was it goes down the drain

671
00:32:44,930 --> 00:32:48,400
从我们的宇宙中消失了
and disappears completely from our universe.

672
00:32:48,400 --> 00:32:51,270
这是对非常神圣的物理定律
This was a fundamental violation

673
00:32:51,270 --> 00:32:55,080
的无情违反
of the most sacred principle of physics.

674
00:32:55,080 --> 00:32:58,550
我本人也被深深震精了
And I was personally, truly shocked.

675
00:33:02,850 --> 00:33:05,920
弗里曼:如果霍金的看法是对的
Freeman: If what Hawking claimed was right,

676
00:33:05,920 --> 00:33:08,960
那就意味则会大多数现代物理
it would mean most of modern physics

677
00:33:08,960 --> 00:33:11,160
都是有瑕疵的
had to be seriously flawed.

678
00:33:11,160 --> 00:33:13,900
黑洞穷其一生都在吞噬星体
Black holes would spend their lives eating stars

679
00:33:13,900 --> 00:33:16,430
却没留下丝毫作案证据
and leave no record of what they'd done.

680
00:33:16,430 --> 00:33:19,770
宇宙中没有别的东西能做到这一点
Nothing else in the universe does this.

681
00:33:19,770 --> 00:33:22,700
原子弹的爆炸也许会使
The fiery blast of a nuclear bomb

682
00:33:22,710 --> 00:33:24,910
视野所及的物质统统气化
might vaporize everything in sight,

683
00:33:24,910 --> 00:33:27,910
但所有的信息仍然存在宇宙之中
but all that information is still in this universe,

684
00:33:27,910 --> 00:33:29,380
无论多么杂乱无章
no matter how scrambled.

685
00:33:29,380 --> 00:33:32,280
根据霍金所说，黑洞
Black holes, according to Hawking,

686
00:33:32,280 --> 00:33:34,420
并不搅乱信息
don't scramble information.

687
00:33:34,420 --> 00:33:37,820
它们摧毁信息
They completely destroy it.

688
00:33:37,820 --> 00:33:39,650
在1981年
That was 1981,

689
00:33:39,660 --> 00:33:42,560
从那之后，我就着了迷了
and from that time forward, I was hooked.

690
00:33:42,560 --> 00:33:45,960
我被黑洞的问题 深深吸引，挥之不去
I could not let go of the question of black holes.

691
00:33:45,960 --> 00:33:49,400
弗里曼: 这一争吵很快就 不是这两个人的事了
Freeman: This squabble soon grows beyond these two men

692
00:33:49,400 --> 00:33:52,470
而是分裂了物理学界
and engulfs all of physics.

693
00:33:52,470 --> 00:33:55,840
赌注不仅仅是胜利者的夸耀
At stake is more than just bragging rights for the winner.

694
00:33:55,840 --> 00:34:00,340
争论的结果会深深影响 我们对宇宙的认识
It turns out to affect the very way we perceive the universe.

695
00:34:08,450 --> 00:34:11,790
银河中大概遍布着
There may be 100 million black holes

696
00:34:11,790 --> 00:34:14,760
100万个黑洞
scattered across the Milky Way.

697
00:34:14,760 --> 00:34:16,390
任何不小心离这些
Anything that strays too close

698
00:34:16,390 --> 00:34:19,300
恒星燃烧残渣太近的物体
to these dark remnants of burned-out stars

699
00:34:19,300 --> 00:34:23,830
都会被巨大的引力场拉进去
will be pulled in by an intense gravitational field.

700
00:34:23,830 --> 00:34:26,670
但是这些落入黑洞的物体身上
But what actually happens

701
00:34:26,670 --> 00:34:29,570
都究竟发生了什么事？
to the stuff that falls into a black hole?

702
00:34:29,570 --> 00:34:32,840
是仅仅简单的抹去
Is it simply wiped out of existence,

703
00:34:32,840 --> 00:34:35,810
还是被黑洞记忆？
or do black holes remember?

704
00:34:35,810 --> 00:34:38,210
这是黑洞战争的
These are the battle lines

705
00:34:38,220 --> 00:34:39,780
战线
of the black-hole war --

706
00:34:39,780 --> 00:34:41,480
挑起这场战争的人
a battle with repercussions

707
00:34:41,490 --> 00:34:43,990
永远无法想象
that the men who started it

708
00:34:43,990 --> 00:34:46,890
战争的后果
could never have imagined.

709
00:34:46,890 --> 00:34:50,260
这是一场在两个伟大思想之间的战争
It's a war between two giant minds.

710
00:34:50,260 --> 00:34:53,700
一边，是著名物理学家霍金
On one side, the famous physicist Stephen Hawking,

711
00:34:53,700 --> 00:34:55,770
另一边，Leonard Susskind
on the other, Leonard Susskind,

712
00:34:55,770 --> 00:34:58,270
弦理论的创始人之一
one of the creators of string theory,

713
00:34:58,270 --> 00:35:01,870
非常困难的物理学分支
a notoriously difficult branch of physics.

714
00:35:01,870 --> 00:35:03,210
霍金认为
Stephen Hawking argues

715
00:35:03,210 --> 00:35:07,110
黑洞摧毁吞噬的一切，无法追溯
black holes destroy what they swallow without a trace.

716
00:35:07,110 --> 00:35:09,910
Leonard Susskind非常的不忿，很不认同
Leonard Susskind passionately disagrees.

717
00:35:09,910 --> 00:35:11,450
10年来
But for 10 years,

718
00:35:11,450 --> 00:35:13,620
他努力寻找霍金关于
he struggled to find anything wrong

719
00:35:13,620 --> 00:35:16,280
黑洞辐射出所吞质量理论中的任何错误
with Hawking's concept of how black holes radiate away the matter they swallow.

720
00:35:16,280 --> 00:35:19,650
在非常非常遥远的地方
It was thought to be inconceivable

721
00:35:19,650 --> 00:35:23,380
在粒子落入黑洞的地方
that somehow the things which fell into the black hole

722
00:35:23,390 --> 00:35:27,290
落入黑洞的物体
could have anything to do with the Hawking radiation,

723
00:35:27,290 --> 00:35:30,420
会产生霍金辐射
which was coming out from very, very far,

724
00:35:30,430 --> 00:35:32,430
这被认为是难以想象的
from where the particles fell in.

725
00:35:32,430 --> 00:35:36,460
弗里曼:于是，他开始为这个问题思考新解释
Freeman: Then he began looking at the problem in a new way.

726
00:35:36,470 --> 00:35:40,000
称之为生-死悖论
Call it the dead-and-alive paradox.

727
00:35:40,000 --> 00:35:42,240
这是个宇宙大脑试验
It's a cosmic thought experiment

728
00:35:42,240 --> 00:35:44,810
从一个宇航员Alice，开始
starring an astronaut named Alice,

729
00:35:44,810 --> 00:35:48,310
她的朋友bob，以及一个黑洞
her friend Bob, and a black hole.

730
00:35:48,310 --> 00:35:51,750
Susskind: Bob在绕黑洞轨道上的太空船里
Susskind: Bob is orbiting the black hole in a spaceship,

731
00:35:51,750 --> 00:35:54,680
Alice打算跳到黑洞里
and Alice decides to jump into the black hole.

732
00:35:54,680 --> 00:35:59,220
Bob 会看到什么？ Alice会看到什么？
What does Bob see, and what does Alice see?

733
00:35:59,220 --> 00:36:02,690
呃，Bob看到Alice向黑洞摔去
Well, Bob sees Alice falling toward the black hole,

734
00:36:02,690 --> 00:36:07,330
越来越接近界限，但越来越慢
getting closer and closer to the horizon, but slowing down.

735
00:36:07,330 --> 00:36:09,930
弗里曼:因为黑洞的引力
Freeman: Because the gravity of the black hole

736
00:36:09,930 --> 00:36:13,600
严重的扭曲了事件界限附近的时空
severely distorts space and time near the event horizon,

737
00:36:13,600 --> 00:36:16,400
爱因斯坦的相对论预测
Einstein's theory of relativity predicts

738
00:36:16,400 --> 00:36:19,670
Bob会看到Alice移动的越来越慢
that Bob will see Alice moving slower and slower,

739
00:36:19,670 --> 00:36:22,540
直到她最终停止
until she eventually stops.

740
00:36:22,540 --> 00:36:25,050
所以，在Bob看来
So, from Bob's point of view,

741
00:36:25,050 --> 00:36:27,580
Alice变得完全静止了
Alice simply becomes completely immobile

742
00:36:27,580 --> 00:36:30,650
脸上带着大大的微笑
with a big smile on her face.

743
00:36:30,650 --> 00:36:32,790
这就是故事的结尾
And that's the end of the story.

744
00:36:32,790 --> 00:36:37,190
对Alice来说，落向黑洞的过程 长过永生永世
It takes forever for Alice to fall through the black hole.

745
00:36:37,190 --> 00:36:38,590
另一方面
On other hand,

746
00:36:38,590 --> 00:36:42,900
Alice却对发生的事情有 完全不同的描述
Alice has a completely different description of what happens.

747
00:36:42,900 --> 00:36:46,200
她顺利穿越了界限
She just falls completely cleanly through the horizon,

748
00:36:46,200 --> 00:36:48,500
不疼不痒，不红不肿
feeling no pain, no bump.

749
00:36:48,500 --> 00:36:51,970
只是在她接近内部时
It's only when she approaches the interior

750
00:36:51,970 --> 00:36:54,110
她才感到不舒服
when she starts to feel uncomfortable.

751
00:36:54,110 --> 00:36:58,210
从那点开始，她感到越来越难受
And at that point, she starts to get more and more distorted,

752
00:36:58,210 --> 00:37:01,120
我不打算详细叙述她的遭遇
and I don't want to go into detail what happens to her.

753
00:37:01,120 --> 00:37:02,420
这可不让人赏心悦目
It's not pretty.

754
00:37:02,420 --> 00:37:04,790
弗里曼: 对于同一事件的两种描述
Freeman: These two descriptions of the same events

755
00:37:04,790 --> 00:37:06,690
显得很奇怪
appear to be at odds.

756
00:37:06,690 --> 00:37:10,590
其一，Alice粘到了时间界限上
In one, Alice is stuck at the event horizon.

757
00:37:10,590 --> 00:37:13,960
另一个，她穿过了界限
In the other, she sails right through.

758
00:37:13,960 --> 00:37:16,900
在一个版本里，她死了
In one version, she dies.

759
00:37:16,900 --> 00:37:21,600
另一个版本， 她被封存在时间里，活着
In the other, she's frozen in time but alive.

760
00:37:21,600 --> 00:37:24,910
但是Leonard Susskind突然意识到
But then Leonard Susskind suddenly realized

761
00:37:24,910 --> 00:37:29,980
如何解决这个悖论，赢得黑洞战争
how to resolve this paradox and win the black-hole war.

762
00:37:29,980 --> 00:37:32,650
呃，我开始认为我们建立弦理论
Well, I began to think that some of the ideas

763
00:37:32,650 --> 00:37:35,350
的某些思想有助于
that we had developed for string theory

764
00:37:35,350 --> 00:37:38,720
解决这个问题，这个悖论
could help resolve this problem, this paradox.

765
00:37:38,720 --> 00:37:41,390
弦理论的一个想法是
One way of thinking about string theory

766
00:37:41,390 --> 00:37:44,390
基本粒子不止我们所见.
is that elementary particles are simply more than meets the eye.

767
00:37:44,390 --> 00:37:46,130
你看到这儿的螺旋桨了吗？
You see this propeller here?

768
00:37:46,130 --> 00:37:49,560
这个螺旋桨—当它以非常非常快 的速度旋转时
This propeller -- when it's spinning very, very rapidly,

769
00:37:49,560 --> 00:37:51,470
你所见的只是中心的轮毂
all you see is the central hub.

770
00:37:51,470 --> 00:37:54,800
这就像是基本粒子
It looks like no more than a simple particle.

771
00:37:54,800 --> 00:37:58,510
如果你有一个非常好的高速照相机
But if you had a really high-speed camera

772
00:37:58,510 --> 00:38:01,780
那就会发现螺旋桨在旋转
that could catch it as it was spinning,

773
00:38:01,780 --> 00:38:04,510
你会发现它比你认为的要复杂
you would discover that there's more to it than you realized.

774
00:38:04,510 --> 00:38:06,450
有浆片
There's the blades.

775
00:38:06,450 --> 00:38:09,250
浆片使它看上去更大
And the blades would make it look bigger.

776
00:38:09,250 --> 00:38:10,620
在弦理论里
In string theory,

777
00:38:10,620 --> 00:38:14,190
基本粒子在振动之上还有振动
an elementary particle has vibrations on top of vibrations.

778
00:38:14,190 --> 00:38:15,960
这就像是说
It's as though this propeller

779
00:38:15,960 --> 00:38:19,860
在螺旋桨的浆片上， 还有更多的螺旋桨
had, on the ends of its blades, more propellers.

780
00:38:19,860 --> 00:38:22,660
这些螺旋桨的叶片上
And those propellers had propellers

781
00:38:22,660 --> 00:38:26,200
还有螺旋桨，无穷无尽
on the ends of their blades, out to infinity,

782
00:38:26,200 --> 00:38:30,040
每个螺旋桨都比上一级的 转的更快
each propeller going faster than the previous one.

783
00:38:30,040 --> 00:38:33,110
如果你能用一个 超高—超高速照相机记录
As you would catch it with a higher- and higher-speed camera,

784
00:38:33,110 --> 00:38:36,540
你就可以看到更多的结构
you would see more and more structure come into focus,

785
00:38:36,540 --> 00:38:38,980
粒子看上去在增长
and the particle would seem to grow.

786
00:38:38,980 --> 00:38:40,580
它会不停的增长
It would grow endlessly

787
00:38:40,580 --> 00:38:43,220
直到它充满整个宇宙
until it filled up the whole universe.

788
00:38:45,750 --> 00:38:48,220
弗里曼: Leonard 意识到
Freeman: Leonard realized

789
00:38:48,220 --> 00:38:51,560
黑洞就像个极高速照相机
that a black hole is like an ultra-high-speed camera.

790
00:38:51,560 --> 00:38:54,360
它看上去使接近事件界限的物体
It appears to slow objects down

791
00:38:54,360 --> 00:38:56,930
运动速度减慢
as they approach the event horizon.

792
00:38:56,930 --> 00:38:59,400
是做另一个大脑试验的时间了
Time for another thought experiment.

793
00:38:59,400 --> 00:39:02,770
黑洞，Bob和Alice回来了
The black hole, Bob, and Alice are back,

794
00:39:02,770 --> 00:39:05,240
但这次，Alice有一架飞机
but this time, Alice has an airplane

795
00:39:05,240 --> 00:39:07,740
由弦理论螺旋桨推进
powered by a string-theory propeller.

796
00:39:07,740 --> 00:39:11,050
对Alice来说，没什么变化
For Alice, not much changes.

797
00:39:11,050 --> 00:39:12,980
她坐在驾驶室里
She sits in the cockpit

798
00:39:12,980 --> 00:39:15,820
正飞过事件界限
and flies right over the event horizon,

799
00:39:15,820 --> 00:39:20,120
只看得到螺旋桨的中央轮毂
all the time seeing just the central hub of her propeller.

800
00:39:20,120 --> 00:39:21,390
她又在黑洞的中心
And she meets the same horrible fate

801
00:39:21,400 --> 00:39:23,330
遭受了同样的恐怖命运
at the heart of the black hole,

802
00:39:23,340 --> 00:39:27,740
这次伴随着一些飞机碎片
this time accompanied by some plane debris.

803
00:39:27,740 --> 00:39:30,440
Bob所见却大为不同
Bob's view is very different.

804
00:39:30,440 --> 00:39:33,580
Susskind: 首先，他看清楚了
Susskind: So, first he sees the first propeller

805
00:39:33,580 --> 00:39:35,050
第一个螺旋桨
come into existence.

806
00:39:35,050 --> 00:39:37,280
然后，时间越来越慢
Then later when it's slowed down even further,

807
00:39:37,280 --> 00:39:39,950
他开始看到更外面的螺旋桨
he begins to see the outer propellers

808
00:39:39,950 --> 00:39:43,560
一个又一个
come into existence sort of one by one.

809
00:39:43,560 --> 00:39:45,960
造成的影响就是
And the effect is for the whole propeller

810
00:39:45,960 --> 00:39:48,460
整个螺旋桨越来越大
to get bigger and bigger and bigger and grow

811
00:39:48,460 --> 00:39:51,460
增长的足够大，能够覆盖整个界限
and eventually be big enough to cover the whole horizon.

812
00:39:57,500 --> 00:40:01,710
弗里曼: 这两个观点不再是不可调和的
Freeman: These two views no longer seem so irreconcilable.

813
00:40:01,710 --> 00:40:05,040
Alice不是在黑洞中心挤个稀巴烂
Alice is either squished at the center of the black hole

814
00:40:05,040 --> 00:40:07,510
就是布满了整个界限
or smeared all over the event horizon.

815
00:40:07,510 --> 00:40:11,550
Leonard给这个新现象取了个名字
Leonard has a name for this new way of seeing things --

816
00:40:11,550 --> 00:40:14,190
全息定理
the holographic principle.

817
00:40:14,190 --> 00:40:17,060
我开始认为 嘿，等一下—
I began to think, hey, wait a minute --

818
00:40:17,060 --> 00:40:20,020
这听上去极其像是全息图
this sounds awfully much like a hologram.

819
00:40:20,030 --> 00:40:22,190
Alice在中心
There's Alice at the center,

820
00:40:22,190 --> 00:40:26,460
如果我看一下— 允许我不称之为界限
and if I look at the -- let me not call it the horizon.

821
00:40:26,470 --> 00:40:28,230
让我称之为表面，薄层
Let me just call it the surface, the film.

822
00:40:28,230 --> 00:40:30,870
你所看到的一切就是 完全搅乱的质量，
All you see is a completely scrambled mess,

823
00:40:30,870 --> 00:40:34,170
它们描绘的是一回事
and somehow they're representing exactly the same thing.

824
00:40:34,170 --> 00:40:35,840
弗里曼: Leonard的想法是 --
Freeman: Leonard's idea --

825
00:40:35,840 --> 00:40:38,340
黑洞的事件界限
that the event horizon of a black hole

826
00:40:38,340 --> 00:40:40,710
是一个三维物体的
is a two-dimensional representation

827
00:40:40,710 --> 00:40:44,080
二维描述 它的中心--
of a three-dimensional object at its center --

828
00:40:44,080 --> 00:40:46,950
信息丢失的问题解决了
solves the problem of information loss.

829
00:40:46,950 --> 00:40:49,890
落入黑洞的所有物体
Every object that falls into a black hole

830
00:40:49,890 --> 00:40:52,790
在质量中心留下记号
leaves its mark both at the central mass

831
00:40:52,790 --> 00:40:56,730
在事件界限留下闪微光的全息图像
and on the shimmering hologram at the event horizon.

832
00:40:56,730 --> 00:40:58,060
当黑洞
When the black hole

833
00:40:58,060 --> 00:41:00,830
从界限发出霍金辐射
emits Hawking radiation from the horizon,

834
00:41:00,830 --> 00:41:05,070
辐射和落进去的东西相联系
that radiation is connected to the stuff that fell in.

835
00:41:05,070 --> 00:41:08,910
信息并未丢失
Information is not lost.

836
00:41:08,910 --> 00:41:13,180
2004年，在都柏林的 一次科学会议上
In 2004 at a scientific conference in Dublin,

837
00:41:13,180 --> 00:41:15,510
霍金承认失败
Hawking conceded defeat.

838
00:41:15,510 --> 00:41:18,820
黑洞并未摧毁信息
Black holes do not destroy information.

839
00:41:18,820 --> 00:41:23,050
Leonard Susskind赢得了黑洞之战
Leonard Susskind had won the black-hole war.

840
00:41:23,060 --> 00:41:25,520
但意义不只如此
But he'd done much more than that

841
00:41:25,520 --> 00:41:29,390
因为这一理论不止应用于黑洞
because the theory does not merely apply to black holes.

842
00:41:29,400 --> 00:41:34,800
它使我们重新审视真实的世界
It forces us to picture all of reality in a new way.

843
00:41:34,800 --> 00:41:36,700
似乎在描述你，我 和这屋子里的一切时
It's as if there were two versions

844
00:41:36,700 --> 00:41:39,600
可以有2个版本
of the description of you and me and what's in this room,

845
00:41:39,610 --> 00:41:41,840
其中之一
one of them being

846
00:41:41,840 --> 00:41:47,680
普通的，视觉上的，三维实体
the normal, perceived, three-dimensional reality

847
00:41:47,680 --> 00:41:50,680
另一个是某种全息图像
and the other being a kind of holographic image

848
00:41:50,680 --> 00:41:53,990
映射在墙上，完全混乱
on the walls of the room, completely scrambled

849
00:41:53,990 --> 00:41:57,760
但仍然保存着同样、准确的信息
but still with the same, exact information in it.

850
00:41:57,760 --> 00:42:01,360
这个想法现在— 那已不再仅是想法了
That idea has now -- it's not an idea anymore.

851
00:42:01,360 --> 00:42:04,560
那是一个真实的基本物理定律
It's a really basic principle of physics

852
00:42:04,560 --> 00:42:09,230
信息保存在宇宙边缘的
that information is stored on a kind of holographic film

853
00:42:09,240 --> 00:42:11,500
某种全息胶片上
at the edges of the universe.

854
00:42:11,500 --> 00:42:13,370
弗里曼: 某种意义上来讲
Freeman: In a sense,

855
00:42:13,370 --> 00:42:17,340
三维空间只是真实的一种版本
three-dimensional space is just one version of reality.

856
00:42:17,340 --> 00:42:21,180
另一版本在一个平的、全息胶片上
The other version exists on a flat, holographic film

857
00:42:21,180 --> 00:42:26,420
位于几十亿光年外的宇宙边界上
billions of light-years away at the edge of the cosmos.

858
00:42:26,420 --> 00:42:29,320
这两种真实为什么同时共存?
Why these two realities seem to coexist

859
00:42:29,320 --> 00:42:32,920
这是目前物理学家 需要解决的最大问题
is now the biggest puzzle physics needs to solve.

860
00:42:32,930 --> 00:42:35,590
最大的挑战之一就是
One of the big challenges that comes out of all of this

861
00:42:35,590 --> 00:42:38,830
理解空间本身
is understanding space itself.

862
00:42:38,830 --> 00:42:41,330
当三维空间的信息
Why is space three-dimensional

863
00:42:41,330 --> 00:42:45,900
都被保存在二维的全息图上时
when all of the information that's stored in that space

864
00:42:45,910 --> 00:42:48,710
空间为什么还要是三维的呢？
is stored as a two-dimensional hologram?

865
00:42:48,710 --> 00:42:50,810
黑洞提升了这些挑战
A black hole raises these challenges

866
00:42:50,810 --> 00:42:52,940
使这些挑战更加尖锐
and really sharpens these challenges

867
00:42:52,950 --> 00:42:54,680
因为，在黑洞里
because it's practically a place

868
00:42:54,680 --> 00:42:57,750
平常的空间不再存在了
where ordinary space doesn't exist anymore.

869
00:42:57,750 --> 00:43:02,020
于是，如果我问空间是怎么显现的
So, if I'm asked questions about how space emerges,

870
00:43:02,020 --> 00:43:05,060
我必须简单的说， 呃，我们正在考虑
I will simply have to say, well, we're thinking about it.

871
00:43:05,060 --> 00:43:07,460
我们并不理解空间
We don't understand it.

872
00:43:08,490 --> 00:43:11,160
几乎一个世纪以来，
Black holes have been a source of fascination

873
00:43:11,160 --> 00:43:12,430
黑洞是魅力之源
for almost a century.

874
00:43:12,430 --> 00:43:15,670
我们曾认为它是时间机器，
We've thought of them as time machines,

875
00:43:15,670 --> 00:43:18,140
通向平行宇宙的捷径
shortcuts to parallel universes,

876
00:43:18,140 --> 00:43:21,740
某一天吞噬地球的怪物
as monsters that will one day devour the Earth.

877
00:43:21,740 --> 00:43:26,010
好，所有这些观点 也许有一天都会成为现实
Well, any of these ideas may turn out to be true one day.

878
00:43:26,010 --> 00:43:28,380
不过此时此地
But right here, right now,

879
00:43:28,380 --> 00:43:32,850
黑洞对你和我有一个 深远的影响
black holes have a profound effect on you and me.

880
00:43:32,850 --> 00:43:36,720
它们闪着微光的全息表面
Their shimmering, holographic surfaces

881
00:43:36,720 --> 00:43:38,690
看上去在告诉我们
seem to be telling us

882
00:43:38,690 --> 00:43:46,850
所有我们认为是在这里的东西，其实是我们神秘宇宙边界的镜像
that everything we think is here is mirrored out there

883
00:43:46,850 --> 00:43:48,430
-- 感谢观赏 --

